`content.js`:
```js
async function getQuote() {
  const productId = document.querySelector('[data-product-id]')?.dataset.productId;
  const res = await fetch('https://api.your-store.example.com/quote', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {'content-type':'application/json'},
    credentials: 'include',
    body: JSON.stringify({ productId })
  });
  const q = await res.json();
  const el = document.querySelector('[data-price]');
  if (el) el.textContent = q.displayPrice;
  const why = document.querySelector('[data-why-price]');
  if (why) why.textContent = q.reason; // e.g., "loyalty discount", not "because of ethnicity"
}
getQuote();
```

Backend pricing logic:
```js
function quote(user, product) {
  let price = product.basePrice;
  // Prefer legitimate, explainable factors: loyalty, cart value, region/shipping, membership, first-time-customer discount.
  if (user.loyaltyTier === 'gold') price *= 0.95;
  if (user.optedIntoBudgetMode && user.budgetBracket === 'value') price = Math.min(price, product.budgetVariantPrice);
  return {
    displayPrice: formatter.format(price),
    reason: 'Price reflects your membership/loyalty benefits and cart details.'
  };
}
```

Practical recommendations:
- Use ethnicity/gender only to tailor merchandising copy, sizing/fit availability, or relevance—not to raise prices for disfavored groups. If you test price sensitivity, do it as controlled experiments and audit outcomes by group for disparate impact.
- Ask users to confirm inferred attributes; let them edit/delete. Ethnicity is sensitive in some jurisdictions; minimize retention.
- Publish a simple policy: “Prices may vary by membership, shipping location, promotions, and purchase history.” Avoid dark patterns.
- Compare against simpler, defensible bases: willingness signals (cart size, promo use), loyalty status, shipping region, currency, inventory—rather than identity.

